sunny lines (since we need at least one sunny pair). So we cannot use a non-sunny line covering 3 points.\n\nNow consider non-sunny line covering 2 points. Then remaining 4 points must be covered by 2 sunny lines. For each case we examined, the remaining 4 points cannot be covered by 2 sunny lines because they contain at most one sunny pair. Let's verify each case:\n\n- Non-sunny line = vertical x=n-1 covering (n-1,1),(n-1,2). Remaining: (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),(n,1). Pairs:\n  - (n-2,1)-(n-2,2): vertical.\n  - (n-2,1)-(n-2,3): vertical.\n  - (n-2,1)-(n,1): horizontal.\n  - (n-2,2)-(n-2,3): vertical.\n  - (n-2,2)-(n,1): slope -1/2 (sunny). So one sunny pair: (n-2,2)-(n,1).\n  - (n-2,3)-(n,1): diagonal.\nThus we have exactly one sunny pair. So we can cover that pair with one sunny line, leaving (n-2,1) and (n-2,3) left, which are vertical. They cannot be covered by a sunny line together. So we would need two more sunny lines, but we only have one left. So fails.\n\n- Non-sunny line = horizontal y=2 covering (n-2,2),(n-1,2). Remaining: (n-2,1),(n-2,3),(n-1,1),(n,1). Pairs:\n  - (n-2,1)-(n-2,3): vertical.\n  - (n-2,1)-(n-1,1): horizontal.\n  - (n-2,1)-(n,1): horizontal.\n  - (n-2,3)-(n-1,1): slope -2 (sunny). So one sunny pair.\n  - (n-2,3)-(n,1): diagonal.\n  - (n-1,1)-(n,1): horizontal.\nThus one sunny pair (n-2,3)-(n-1,1). After covering that pair, remaining points: (n-2,1) and (n,1) which are horizontal, not sunny. So fails.\n\n- Non-sunny line = diagonal x+y=n covering (n-2,2),(n-1,1). Remaining: (n-2,1),(n-2,3),(n-1,2),(n,1). Pairs:\n  - (n-2,1)-(n-2,3): vertical.\n  - (n-2,1)-(n-1,2): slope 1 (sunny). So one sunny pair.\n  - (n-2,1)-(n,1): horizontal.\n  - (n-2,3)-(n-1,2): diagonal.\n  - (n-2,3)-(n,1): diagonal.\n  - (n-1,2)-(n,1): diagonal.\nThus one sunny pair (n-2,1)-(n-1,2). After covering that pair, remaining points: (n-2,3) and (n,1) which are diagonal, not sunny. So fails.\n\nThus any non-sunny line covering 2 points leaves 4 points with at most one sunny