t only sunny slopes:\n\nFor each point (x,y), we have slopes to each A_i:\n\n- B1 (2,1): slopes: to A1: 0 (non-sunny), to A2: -1 (non-sunny), to A3: -2 (sunny), to A4: -3 (sunny).\n- B2 (2,2): slopes: to A1: 1 (sunny), to A2: 0 (non-sunny), to A3: -1 (non-sunny), to A4: -2 (sunny).\n- B3 (2,3): slopes: to A1: 2 (sunny), to A2: 1 (sunny), to A3: 0 (non-sunny), to A4: -1 (non-sunny).\n- C1 (3,1): slopes: to A1: 0 (non-sunny), to A2: -0.5 (sunny), to A3: -1 (non-sunny), to A4: -1.5 (sunny).\n- C2 (3,2): slopes: to A1: 0.5 (sunny), to A2: 0 (non-sunny), to A3: -0.5 (sunny), to A4: -1 (non-sunny).\n- D1 (4,1): slopes: to A1: 0 (non-sunny), to A2: -1/3 (sunny), to A3: -2/3 (sunny), to A4: -1 (non-sunny).\n\nThus we have a bipartite graph between points and lines (i=1..4) where an edge exists if the slope is sunny. We need to assign each point to a line such that each line gets a consistent slope (i.e., all assigned points to line i must have the same slope). So we need to choose for each line i a slope value from the set of slopes that appear for that line among the points assigned to it, and assign points accordingly.\n\nLet's list for each line i the possible slopes (sunny) and which points correspond to each slope:\n\nLine i=1 (A1):\n- slope 1: point B2\n- slope 2: point B3\n- slope 0.5: point C2\n\nLine i=2 (A2):\n- slope 1: point B3\n- slope -0.5: point C1\n- slope -1/3: point D1\n\nLine i=3 (A3):\n- slope -2: point B1\n- slope -0.5: point C2\n- slope -2/3: point D1\n\nLine i=4 (A4):\n- slope -3: point B1\n- slope -2: point B2\n- slope -1.5: point C1\n\nThus each line has three possible slopes (sunny) each associated with a specific point.\n\nNow we need to assign each point to exactly one line, and each line can have multiple points as long as they share the same slope. So for each line i, we can pick at most one slope (since if we pick a slope, we can assign all points that have that slope to that line). But we cannot assign points with different slopes to the same